        {
            "number": 4,
            "revision": "1bed28f6a765efb82af42acfa09a771678160ead",
            "parents": [
                "95a2fa58dd54c53126c7c5bcb1a033b850c92341"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/73/84573/4",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Sora",
                "email": "sora.suu.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                "username": "baxiche"
            },
            "createdOn": 1761539916,
            "author": {
                "name": "Sora",
                "email": "sora.suu.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                "username": "baxiche"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Don't make this a Yv4-specific change.  If we should do a lazy-remount instead of an unmount, propose that in the common file."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 15,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Sora",
                        "email": "sora.suu.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "baxiche"
                    },
                    "message": "- I don't think there is any need to remount the file-system to read-only before execute the sled power cycle.\n\n- BusyBox\u2019s mount implementation simply doesn\u2019t provide any lazy remount option."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-yosemite4/recipes-phosphor/state/phosphor-state-manager/chassis-powercycle",
                    "line": 211,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why wouldn't we try a readonly remount first and if that fails then do a lazy unmount?\n\nDoesn't an unmount potentially cause issues where daemons can no longer write to regions of the flash they expected to?  Why is a lazy unmount explicitly better than a readonly remount?  Is there such a thing as a \"lazy remount\"?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-yosemite4/recipes-phosphor/state/phosphor-state-manager/chassis-powercycle",
                    "line": 211,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Sora",
                        "email": "sora.suu.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "baxiche"
                    },
                    "message": "Firstly, remounting the persist filesystem as read-only actually increases the likelihood of flash memory corruption on our Yosemite4 system, as mentioned in the commit message.\n\nCould I ask what issue the remount is intended to address, and why you prefer to keep it? I think we need to be aligned on these points before we can properly discuss this patch."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-yosemite4/recipes-phosphor/state/phosphor-state-manager/chassis-powercycle",
                    "line": 211,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> Firstly, remounting the persist filesystem as read-only actually increases the likelihood of flash memory corruption on our Yosemite4 system, as mentioned in the commit message.\n\nThe commit message had a pretty weak assertion:\n    Testing indicates it can even increase the likelihood of corruption.\n\nDo you have data to back this up?  What is the probability of corruption with a read-only remount and a probability of corruption without a read-only remount?\n\nIntuitively, I have no idea why a read-only remount would make corruption _more likely_ than doing nothing.  In practice, maybe your data is correct, but I just have no idea why that would be the case.  Do you have any ideas why?\n\n> Could I ask what issue the remount is intended to address, and why you prefer to keep it? I think we need to be aligned on these points before we can properly discuss this patch.\n\nAs I mentioned on Discord, the contributor claimed that they saw better corruption numbers.  I similarly didn't press them on it because it seemed intuitive that it _might_ be better and at least _shouldn't_ be worse."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-yosemite4/recipes-phosphor/state/phosphor-state-manager/chassis-powercycle",
                    "line": 211,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Sora",
                        "email": "sora.suu.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "baxiche"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi @patrick@stwcx.xyz, the read-only remount actually performed worse in our tests. You can check the ticket I mentioned on Discord."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-yosemite4/recipes-phosphor/state/phosphor-state-manager/chassis-powercycle",
                    "line": 211,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Sora",
                        "email": "sora.suu.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "baxiche"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi @patrick@stwcx.xyz, just checking if there\u2019s any update on your side. Thanks."
                }
            ],
